        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nI want Liquid Glass on every container in the app so it looks modern throughout.",
          "label": "evals/swiftui-liquid-glass/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "3306ad0499dd45014d35ce7ac7dd954045db70c16bb5b92314c8f9089bb8847e",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "I couldn't read the app's code. Shell access is disabled here, and the IDE tools only cover other projects, so I haven't changed anything. I'd also advise against glass on every container.\n\nLiquid Glass is meant for the controls and navigation layer that floats above content: toolbars, tab bars, floating buttons, chips, sheets. If you put it on cards, list rows and backgrounds, glass ends up layered on glass. Legibility drops, the blur costs frame rate, and the hierarchy flattens, so nothing reads as floating anymore.\n\nWhat I'd do instead:\n- **Use system chrome first.** Building with the iOS 26 SDK gives you glass on `NavigationStack` toolbars, `TabView`, sheets and standard buttons without code changes.\n- **Add `.glassEffect()` to custom floating controls.** That means custom bars, chips and overlays. Apply it after the layout and appearance modifiers, and group neighbors in a `GlassEffectContainer`.\n- **Use `.buttonStyle(.glass)` or `.glassProminent` for actions.** Add `.interactive()` only on tappable elements.\n- **Gate with `#available(iOS 26, *)`.** Fall back to `.ultraThinMaterial` on earlier versions.\n- **Leave content containers opaque.** Cards, rows and full-screen backgrounds keep their current styling.\n\nIf you still want it on every container, tell me and I'll do it. A reusable `.glassCard()` modifier with the availability fallback would make that a one-line change per view.\n\nI need the app's source in a path I can read, or the path of the SwiftUI files, to audit where each of these applies."
        },
        "score": 1,
        "success": true,
        "testCase": {
          "description": "overuse",
          "vars": {
            "request": "file:///private/tmp/b6/evals/swiftui-liquid-glass/cases/overuse.txt"
          },
          "assert": [
            {
              "type": "javascript",
              "metric": "restraint",
              "value": "if (!/not every|sparing|overuse|avoid|selective|hierarch|too much|legib/i.test(output)) throw new Error('pushes back on applying glass everywhere');\nreturn true;\n"
